-names>VA</given-names></name><name><surname>Dang</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Engler</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Zannettino</surname><given-names>ACW</given-names></name><name><surname>Cambareri</surname><given-names>A</given-names></name><name><surname>Quinn</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Manley</surname><given-names>P</given-names></name><name><surname>Hughes</surname><given-names>TP</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Oct-1 mediated influx is a key determinant of the intracellular uptake of imatinib but not AMN107; reduced OCT-1 activity is the cause of low <italic>in vitro</italic> sensitivity to imatinib</article-title><source>Blood</source><year>2006</year><volume>107</volume>(in press)</citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><fig id="fig1"><label>Figure 1</label><caption><p>Structures of Abl kinase (<bold>A</bold>) in the active (<xref ref-type="other" rid="bib7">Fendrich <italic>et al</italic>, 2006</xref>) and (<bold>C</bold>) inactive states, with dasatinib (blue) docked and nilotinib (magenta) as bound in the crystal structure (<xref ref-type="other" rid="bib32">Weisberg <italic>et al</italic>, 2005</xref>), respectively. The differing conformations of the glycine-rich or P-loop (yellow) and the activation loop (green) are induced or stabilised by the different binding modes of the two inhibitors. (<bold>B</bold>) shows a superposition of the two distinct conformations, emphasising how dasatinib and nilotinib occupy different parts of the cleft between the N- (upper) and C-terminal (lower) lobes of the kinase. The corresponding aspects of the molecular structures of (<bold>D</bold>) dasatinib and (<bold>E</bold>) nilotinib are depicted, with their respective H-bond interactions with the Abl kinase domain indicated in red, in comparison to imatinib (<bold>F</bold>).</p></caption><graphic xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" mime-subtype="jpe